       <p><strong>Expect the casino and shops to close while docked.</strong> Ships close their casinos and duty-free retail alongside — that's Bahamian port rules, not NCL stinginess — and reporting from the July 31 extended call noted exactly those closures. On a four-night cruise where the ship now spends most of two days at a pier, that's a real chunk of casino and shopping time gone. Restaurants, bars, pools, and shows run normally.</p>
 
-      <p><strong>Great Stirrup Cay until 8 p.m. is a quiet upgrade.</strong> Private-island days usually end mid-afternoon; a 12-hour call means the beach crowd thins after the usual departure hour and you get the island in early-evening light. One honest caveat: NCL's new Great Tides Waterpark at Great Stirrup Cay is slated to open in September 2026 — after this sailing — so the extended day buys more beach and lagoon time, not the new slides.</p>
